course: dictionary
lesson: 3786
title: Words of freedom
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a whole group of words works as one meaning. «Footloose and fancy-free» is a phrase, and its clues tell you what it means. Look at the words around it before you decide.
explain: Footloose means free to go where you like and do what you want, with no responsibilities to tie you down. Picture a kite that has been let go of its string: it can drift wherever the wind takes it.
explain: The phrase «footloose and fancy-free» means single and free of ties. Ties are the people and jobs that hold you back. A person who is footloose and fancy-free has nobody and nothing stopping them from travelling or choosing what to do next.
fact: Footloose means free to go where you like and do what you want.
fact: Informally, footloose can also mean not settled in one place.
fact: «Footloose and fancy-free» means single and free of ties.
fact: Ties here means people or responsibilities, such as a family or a job, that hold you back.
fact: A quick check: swap in the word free and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have a good clue.
exercise: What does footloose mean? | Wet and grey | Stuck in one place with no way out | *Free to go where you like and do what you want | Very tired after a long walk
exercise: What does the phrase «footloose and fancy-free» describe? | A person who is always late for school | A person who is very hungry | A person who loves to cook big meals | *A person free of ties, with no family or job holding them back
exercise: Which sentence shows someone who is footloose? | The dog sat by the fire all day | *He is backpacking round the world with no fixed plans | They counted the apples in the bowl | She has a job and three children at home
test: Informally, footloose can mean... | Very hungry | Wet and grey | Giving out light | *Not settled in one place
test: Which word could replace footloose in «The young couple were ___ and free to travel»? | *Free | Gloomy | Tired | Hungry
exam: Which best explains the meaning of footloose in «footloose and fancy-free»? | Very old and worn out | Full of loose stones | Short and rude to everyone | *Free of ties and able to go where they like
